   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to requiring the office\nfor people with developmental disabilities to purchase and provide essential\npersonal protection equipment to all direct support professionals, care\nrecipients and group home providers in the event of a declaration of a state\nemergency that relates to public health",
    "basePrintNo": "S6295",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6295-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13.07, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6295\n\nSPONSOR: MANNION\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to requiring the\noffice for people with developmental disabilities to purchase and\nprovide essential personal protection equipment to all direct support\nprofessionals, care recipients and group home providers in the event of\na declaration of a state emergency that relates to public health\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation requires that the Office for People with Developmental\nDisabilities (OPWDD) be responsible for purchasing and providing\npersonal protective equipment (PPE) to all direct support professionals,\ncare recipients, and group home providers during a state of emergency\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. Requires OPWDD in the event of a state emergency for public\nhealth to:\n\nPurchase and provide personal protective equipment\n\nConsider best clinical practices that may result in increased need\n\nProvide PPE within 30 days of a declaration\n\nDistribute PPE to direct service professionals, individuals with I/DD,\nand other personnel for a 60 day supply equal to the average daily rate\nduring the height of the COVID-19 pandemic\n\nReplace and replenish PPE as needed and defines damaged PPE\n\nSection 2. Effective Date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation requires the OPWDD to purchase and distribute PPE to\nall direct support professionals, care recipients, and group home\nproviders across the State to ensure that such individuals receive\nenough PPE, which will be replenished as needed, to protect staff and\nresidents during a state of emergency that relates to public health.\n\nDuring the height of the COVID-19 pandemic, voluntary providers were\nleft on their own to acquire PPE. Due to the nature of services, volun-\ntary providers could not wait or hold off services to individuals in\ncongregate settings requiring 24 hour care.  Specifically, small volun-\ntary providers had the most trouble obtaining PPE for their residents\nand staff.  Unlike these small providers, OPWDD and the State will be\nable to leverage their massive size to negotiate better prices for PPE\nand quicker deliveries.\n\nIndividuals with disabilities often have comorbidities which make them\nmore susceptible to infectious viruses like COVID-19. The delay in\nobtaining PPE for many congregate care facilities led to unnecessary\nexposure and risk. It is the State's responsibility to care for this\nvulnerable population and ensure their safety while within the OPWDD\nsystem. By requiring OPWDD to purchase and distribute PPE to individuals\nunder their care, and their staff, this legislation will prevent similar\noccurrences during future public health state of emergencies.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nTBD.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
